      "problem": "Consider the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}+8,\\,{a}_{1}=4,\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nSuppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=4(2n-1)\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & ={{field-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-2}}, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{k}={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{a}_{k+1}={{field-4}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${a}_{n}=4(2n-1)$ for all $n\\in N.$",
      "worked_solution": "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=4(2n-1)\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base case. To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side. \n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1):\\,{a}_{1} & =4(2\\cdot 1-1) \\\\  & =4(2-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the right-hand side and show that it equals the left-hand side.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{k}=4(2k-1).\n$$\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1} & =4(2(k+1)-1) \\\\  & =4(2k+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation using the recurrence relation and inductive hypothesis to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${a}_{n}=4(2n-1)$ for all $n\\in N.$",
